# C# style

Most of this is enforced mechanically by `.editorconfig` +
`EnforceCodeStyleInBuild=true` (violations are build errors, not just IDE
squiggles) — if `dotnet build` is clean, you're already compliant. A few
things worth knowing explicitly:

- File-scoped namespaces everywhere (`namespace Foo.Bar;`), except
  `src/PickMeWhatToListen.Infrastructure/Migrations/**` — EF Core scaffolds
  those with block-scoped namespaces and they're not hand-edited; that
  folder has its own `.editorconfig` override, don't "fix" it.
- `Nullable` is enabled repo-wide. Don't suppress warnings with `!` unless
  you've actually proven non-null at that point (and prefer a comment when
  it's not obvious why).
- Prefer primary constructors for simple dependency-holding classes (see
  `ArtistCatalogService`, `EfArtistRepository`) over explicit constructors
  with backing fields.
- Prefer immutable read-only view models / DTOs (e.g. `ArtistRowViewModel`)
  over `ObservableObject` when the object is just a display snapshot
  rebuilt wholesale on every reload — reserve `[ObservableProperty]` for
  values that actually change in place (see `.cursor/rules/mvvm-wpf.mdc`).
- Don't add comments that narrate what the code does. Comments should
  explain non-obvious *why* (invariants, gotchas, workarounds for library
  limitations) — see `ArtistConfiguration.cs` for the kind of comment that
  earns its place.
- **New files scaffolded by Visual Studio/`dotnet new` tooling (WPF
  code-behind, `dotnet ef migrations add` output) are often saved as UTF-8
  *with* BOM**, but the repo's `.editorconfig` requires `charset = utf-8`
  (no BOM). This passes `dotnet build` silently (charset isn't a Roslyn
  analyzer) but fails `dotnet format --verify-no-changes` — the exact check
  CI runs — with a `CHARSET` error, only discovered the first time CI
  actually runs, not at scaffolding time. Run `dotnet format whitespace`
  after generating any new file this way, before committing.
- **`const` fields need their own naming rule**, separate from the general
  private-fields rule (`dotnet_naming_rule.private_fields_underscore` in
  `.editorconfig`) — otherwise `IDE1006` fires because `const` matches the
  "field" symbol kind but shouldn't take the `_camelCase` style meant for
  instance fields. See `dotnet_naming_rule.constant_fields_pascal_case` in
  `.editorconfig`; rule precedence between the two is resolved automatically
  by specificity (Roslyn orders by modifier/accessibility/symbol subset, not
  file order — no need to reorder them by hand if you add a third case).
- **`string.Normalize()`/`CharUnicodeInfo` need `InvariantGlobalization=false`
  to actually do anything.** Under invariant globalization mode,
  `string.Normalize(...)` silently returns its input unchanged instead of
  throwing — there's no compile- or even test-time signal that it's a
  no-op, only wrong runtime output. This is why the repo runs with
  `InvariantGlobalization=false` (see `Directory.Build.props`); relevant
  again if you touch `ArtistNameNormalizer`, which relies on Unicode NFD
  decomposition to strip diacritics.
